  "id": "DrugBank_DB00620 - MONDO_43455",
  "verbalization": "Tiamcinolone, a corticosteroid, is associated with Humoral Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HHM) through a mechanism involving its use as an indication for conditions that lead to increased hydrocortisone acetate use. The evidence suggests that Triamcinolone is linked to HHM via Hydrocortisone Acetate, which is indicated in multiple conditions, including Stevens-Johnson Syndrome, Sarcoidosis, and Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS)-blocker-induced Angioedema. These conditions are often associated with increased corticosteroid use, which can elevate calcium levels in the blood. The shared pathway from Triamcinolone to Hydrocortisone Acetate, and from there to HHM, highlights a potential mechanism where corticosteroids, when used in multiple conditions, may contribute to the development of HHM. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of corticosteroids in the pathogenesis of HHM, particularly in the context of elevated hydrocortisone acetate levels.",
